{"from": "claim:mk-12-29-shema-quoted-in-full", "type": "mentions", "to": "person:jesus-of-nazareth"}
{"from": "claim:mk-12-29-shema-quoted-in-full", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-22-36-37-greatest-commandment", "note": "Mark's Jesus opens with the full Shema ('Hear, Israel, the Lord our God, the Lord is one', Deuteronomy 6:4) before the love command; Matthew's answer begins with the love command (Deuteronomy 6:5) alone."}
{"from": "claim:mk-12-32-34-scribe-wise-answer", "type": "mentions", "to": "person:jesus-of-nazareth"}
{"from": "claim:mk-12-32-34-scribe-wise-answer", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-22-36-37-greatest-commandment", "note": "The scribe's affirmation and Jesus' 'not far from the kingdom of God' are Mark-only; Matthew 22:40 ends the episode with Jesus' summary and no reply from the lawyer."}
{"from": "claim:mk-12-38-39-scribes-honor-garments", "type": "mentions", "to": "person:jesus-of-nazareth"}
{"from": "claim:mk-12-38-39-scribes-honor-garments", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-23-06-chief-seats", "note": "Mark's warning names long robes and greetings in the marketplaces (Matthew 23:5-7) plus best seats in synagogues (Matthew 23:6); the best-seats claim is shared, the robe detail is Mark's emphasis here."}
{"from": "claim:mk-12-40-devour-widows-houses", "type": "mentions", "to": "person:jesus-of-nazareth"}
{"from": "claim:mk-12-41-treasury-chests", "type": "mentions", "to": "person:jesus-of-nazareth"}
{"from": "claim:mk-12-41-treasury-chests", "type": "mentions", "to": "place:temple-mount"}
{"from": "claim:mk-12-42-two-lepta-quadrans", "type": "mentions", "to": "person:jesus-of-nazareth"}
{"from": "claim:mk-12-44-widow-gave-all", "type": "mentions", "to": "person:jesus-of-nazareth"}
{"from": "artifact:qumran-tefillin", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-12-29-shema-quoted-in-full", "grade": "B", "note": "Tefillin dating from the 1st century CE were discovered at Qumran, and the tefillin scrolls carry four passages including 'Hear, O Israel...' (Deuteronomy 6:4-9) - the passage Mark 12:29 quotes, from the same region and period. The fetched page says '1st century CE'; the B grade rests on that wording."}
{"from": "artifact:nash-papyrus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-12-29-shema-quoted-in-full", "grade": "D", "note": "A c. 150-100 BCE papyrus containing the first part of the Shema Yisrael prayer - but it is 'of unknown provenance', acquired on the Egyptian antiquities market in 1902, and no cited source reports scientific authentication, so the binding calibration rule gives D."}
{"from": "artifact:seat-of-moses-chorazin", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-12-38-39-scribes-honor-garments", "grade": "C", "note": "A carved honorary 'Seat of Moses' found in the Chorazin synagogue shows the custom of special honor seating in Galilean synagogues that 'best seats in the synagogues' presupposes; the synagogue's phases are late 3rd-6th century, well after the 30s CE, hence C."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-shekalim-6-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-12-41-treasury-chests", "grade": "B", "note": "Shekalim 6:1 records thirteen trumpet-shaped collection chests (shofarot) in the Temple court 'into which were placed the shekels that were collected' - the treasury into which Mark 12:41-42 has worshipers cast money, the specific institution in the specific place. Redacted c. 200 CE, so it may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly."}
{"from": "artifact:widows-mite-coin", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-12-42-two-lepta-quadrans", "grade": "B", "note": "The bronze lepton, called prutah in Hebrew, minted under Alexander Jannaeus (103-76 BC), is reported 'still in circulation at the time of Jesus' and was 'the smallest and least valuable coin in circulation in Judea' - the exact coin type Mark 12:42 names (lepta)."}
{"from": "artifact:quadrans-coin", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mk-12-42-two-lepta-quadrans", "grade": "C", "note": "A low-value Roman bronze coin worth one quarter of an as, produced sporadically into the 2nd century CE; the same grade the Matthew 5:26 runner gave. It confirms the Roman small-coin unit the two lepta equal, not the Judean exchange rate itself."}
